{%- comment -%}
  theinmag-legal-accordions
  Six-item accordion stack for the combined legal page (kid version,
  child safety, submission terms, T+Cs, privacy, GDPR). Reuses the
  homepage FAQ accordion pattern: H3 + button + aria-expanded +
  data-open max-height transition (per page-playbook section 8.5).

  Differences from the homepage FAQ:
  - Single-open behaviour (one panel open at a time). Legal page is
    long-read, not cross-comparison; one open keeps focus.
  - Per-item URL hash deep-linking. JotForm consent + checkout +
    footer all link directly to specific anchors.
  - Yellow-star marker on the "kid version" item, since it is the
    one item we explicitly invite kids to open first.
  - Prefers-reduced-motion: instant show/hide, no slide.

  Spec: prompt from session 2026-05-09. Tokens: ../theinmag-docs/theinmag-design-tokens.md.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign blocks = section.blocks | where: 'type', 'accordion_item'

  assign section_style = '--legal-acc-pt: ' | append: section.settings.padding_top | append: 'px; --legal-acc-pb: ' | append: section.settings.padding_bottom | append: 'px;'
-%}

<section
  class="theinmag-legal-accordions"
  data-theinmag-legal-accordions
  aria-label="theINmag policies"
  style="{{ section_style }}"
>
  <div class="theinmag-legal-accordions__inner">

    {%- if blocks.size > 0 -%}
      <ul class="theinmag-legal-accordions__list" role="list">
        {%- for block in blocks -%}
          {%- liquid
            assign title = block.settings.title
            assign anchor_id = block.settings.anchor_id | default: block.id
            assign is_kid = block.settings.is_kid_version
            assign content = block.settings.content
            assign panel_id = 'theinmag-legal-panel-' | append: anchor_id
          -%}
          <li
            class="theinmag-legal-accordions__item{% if is_kid %} is-kid-version{% endif %}"
            id="{{ anchor_id }}"
            {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
          >
            <h2 class="theinmag-legal-accordions__heading">
              <button
                type="button"
                class="theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger"
                aria-expanded="false"
                aria-controls="{{ panel_id }}"
                data-theinmag-legal-trigger
                data-anchor="{{ anchor_id }}"
              >
                {%- if is_kid -%}
                  <span class="theinmag-legal-accordions__star" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" focusable="false">
                      <path d="M12 2.5l2.7 6.1 6.6.6-5 4.6 1.5 6.5L12 17l-5.8 3.3 1.5-6.5-5-4.6 6.6-.6z" fill="currentColor"/>
                    </svg>
                  </span>
                {%- endif -%}
                <span class="theinmag-legal-accordions__title">{{ title }}</span>
                <span class="theinmag-legal-accordions__caret" aria-hidden="true"></span>
              </button>
            </h2>
            <div
              id="{{ panel_id }}"
              class="theinmag-legal-accordions__panel"
              data-theinmag-legal-panel
              data-open="false"
            >
              <div class="theinmag-legal-accordions__content">
                {{ content }}
              </div>
            </div>
          </li>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </ul>
    {%- endif -%}

  </div>
</section>

{%- stylesheet -%}
  /* ================================================================
     theinmag-legal-accordions
     Cream-pair surface continuing from the hero. Single-column,
     edge-to-edge accordion list. Touch-target generous on mobile
     (>=56px header height); content max-width 65ch inside open
     panels so long legal paragraphs do not sprawl on desktop.
     ================================================================ */

  .theinmag-legal-accordions {
    background-color: var(--color-cream, #FBF6EA);
    color: var(--color-inky-purple-navy, #2A1F3D);
    padding: var(--legal-acc-pt, 32px) clamp(0px, 2vw, 16px) var(--legal-acc-pb, 64px);
    position: relative;
  }

  @media (min-width: 750px) {
    .theinmag-legal-accordions {
      padding-left: clamp(16px, 4vw, 32px);
      padding-right: clamp(16px, 4vw, 32px);
    }
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__inner {
    max-width: 880px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ================================================================
     List - thin border between rows. Top + bottom borders frame the
     stack so it reads as a single editorial unit.
     ================================================================ */

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(42, 31, 61, 0.12);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(42, 31, 61, 0.12);
    /* Direct browser anchor jumps clear the sticky header. */
    scroll-margin-top: 96px;
  }

  /* H2 wrapper carries semantic weight (each accordion is a top-
     level policy section, hence H2 not H3). Reset margins/font so
     the button child does the visible heavy lifting. */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__heading {
    margin: 0;
    font: inherit;
  }

  /* ================================================================
     Trigger - full-row button. Star (kid version only) + title on
     the left, caret on the right. min-height: 56px on mobile, 64px
     on desktop so the touch target stays comfortable.
     ================================================================ */

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger {
    appearance: none;
    background: none;
    border: 0;
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(10px, 1.5vw, 14px);
    padding: clamp(18px, 2.4vw, 24px) clamp(16px, 2.5vw, 28px);
    cursor: pointer;
    text-align: left;
    color: var(--color-inky-purple-navy, #2A1F3D);
    min-height: 56px;
    transition: color var(--transition-standard, 0.15s ease),
                background-color var(--transition-standard, 0.15s ease);
  }

  @media (min-width: 750px) {
    .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger {
      min-height: 64px;
    }
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger:hover {
    background-color: rgba(232, 222, 239, 0.35);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px dashed var(--color-purple-dark, #5D3A7A);
    outline-offset: -2px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Active state - heading colour shifts to dark purple, caret
     rotates 180deg (rule below). */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger[aria-expanded="true"] {
    color: var(--color-purple-dark, #5D3A7A);
  }

  /* Star marker for the kid-version item. Yellow stamp accent
     (allowed - this is a small badge moment, not a background field). */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__star {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    color: var(--color-yellow, #F9C23C);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__star svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    /* Subtle deeper-yellow stroke so the star reads on cream
       without losing the warm yellow fill. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 1px 0 rgba(212, 160, 46, 0.6));
  }

  /* Editorial register - Inter 700 (mirrors field-notes article and
     post-card titles). Post Regular is reserved for the H1 at the
     top of the page; stacking six Post Regular triggers reads kiddy
     and tips the page over the Post Regular fatigue line (see
     page-playbook section 7 anti-pattern). */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__title {
    flex: 1;
    font-family: var(--font-body, 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(17px, 1.9vw, 21px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  /* Caret - same chevron style as theinmag-faq for visual unity. */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 22px;
    height: 22px;
    position: relative;
    transition: transform 0.3s ease;
    color: var(--color-purple-dark, #5D3A7A);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret::before,
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    width: 12px;
    height: 2px;
    background-color: currentColor;
    border-radius: 2px;
    transform-origin: 50% 50%;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret::before {
    left: 1px;
    transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(45deg);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret::after {
    right: 1px;
    transform: translateY(-50%) rotate(-45deg);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger[aria-expanded="true"] .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret {
    transform: rotate(180deg);
  }

  /* ================================================================
     Panel - max-height 0 -> 4000px transition (legal copy is
     significantly longer than the FAQ answers, so the safe upper
     bound is bumped). Background flips white when open so the open
     content separates visually from the cream surface.
     ================================================================ */

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__panel {
    max-height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: max-height 0.4s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1),
                background-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__panel[data-open="true"] {
    max-height: 4000px;
    background-color: #FFFFFF;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content {
    padding: clamp(8px, 1.5vw, 16px) clamp(16px, 2.5vw, 28px) clamp(24px, 3vw, 32px);
    max-width: 65ch;
    font-family: var(--font-body, 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.4vw, 16px);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--color-inky-purple-navy, #2A1F3D);
  }

  /* Tighter side padding on mobile so legal copy gets the full
     screen width to breathe. */
  @media (max-width: 749px) {
    .theinmag-legal-accordions__content {
      padding-left: clamp(12px, 4vw, 20px);
      padding-right: clamp(12px, 4vw, 20px);
    }
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content p {
    margin: 0 0 clamp(12px, 1.5vw, 16px);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* H3 inside an open panel - editorial sub-heading. Inter 600
     dark purple, tighter line-height than body. Not Post Regular -
     stacking three-plus Post lockups inside a single open panel
     would read kiddy on a legal page (page-playbook anti-pattern:
     Post Regular fatigue). */
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-body, 'Inter', system-ui, sans-serif);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(16px, 1.7vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--color-purple-dark, #5D3A7A);
    margin: clamp(20px, 2.5vw, 28px) 0 clamp(6px, 0.8vw, 10px);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content h3:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content ul,
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content ol {
    margin: clamp(8px, 1vw, 12px) 0 clamp(12px, 1.5vw, 16px);
    padding-left: clamp(20px, 2vw, 26px);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content li {
    margin: 0 0 clamp(4px, 0.6vw, 6px);
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content strong {
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.005em;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content a {
    color: var(--color-purple-dark, #5D3A7A);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    transition: color 0.15s ease, text-decoration-thickness 0.15s ease;
  }

  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content a:hover,
  .theinmag-legal-accordions__content a:focus-visible {
    color: var(--color-inky-purple-navy, #2A1F3D);
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }

  /* ================================================================
     Reduced motion - state changes happen instantly. No slide, no
     caret rotation animation, no background fade. Keeps the page
     functional for users who have requested no motion.
     ================================================================ */

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .theinmag-legal-accordions__panel,
    .theinmag-legal-accordions__caret,
    .theinmag-legal-accordions__trigger {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
{%- endstylesheet -%}

<script>
  (function () {
    var root = document.querySelector('[data-theinmag-legal-accordions]');
    if (!root) return;

    var triggers = root.querySelectorAll('[data-theinmag-legal-trigger]');
    if (!triggers.length) return;

    var prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
       Single-open accordion. Opening one collapses any others. Per
       prompt: legal copy is long-read, one-at-a-time keeps focus.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
    function setOpen(trigger, open) {
      var panelId = trigger.getAttribute('aria-controls');
      var panel = panelId ? document.getElementById(panelId) : null;
      if (!panel) return;
      trigger.setAttribute('aria-expanded', open ? 'true' : 'false');
      panel.setAttribute('data-open', open ? 'true' : 'false');
    }

    function closeAll() {
      triggers.forEach(function (t) { setOpen(t, false); });
    }

    function openTrigger(trigger) {
      var willOpen = trigger.getAttribute('aria-expanded') !== 'true';
      closeAll();
      if (willOpen) setOpen(trigger, true);
    }

    triggers.forEach(function (trigger) {
      trigger.addEventListener('click', function () {
        openTrigger(trigger);
      });
    });

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
       Sticky-header-aware scrollTo. Subtracts the theinmag-header
       height plus a 12px breath so the open panel's heading lands
       just below the sticky nav. Pattern lifted from page-playbook
       section 8.4.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
    function scrollToItem(item) {
      var header = document.querySelector('.theinmag-header-section') || document.querySelector('header[role="banner"]');
      var headerH = header ? header.offsetHeight : 0;
      var top = item.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.pageYOffset - headerH - 12;
      window.scrollTo({
        top: Math.max(0, top),
        behavior: prefersReducedMotion ? 'auto' : 'smooth'
      });
    }

    /* ----------------------------------------------------------------
       Deep-link on load. If the URL hash matches an item's anchor
       id, open that panel and scroll to it. Used by JotForm consent
       (#submission-terms), checkout (#terms), footer (page root).
       Wait one frame so layout has settled before measuring scroll.
       ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
    function openFromHash() {
      var hash = window.location.hash ? window.location.hash.replace('#', '') : '';
      if (!hash) return;
      var item = document.getElementById(hash);
      if (!item) return;
      var trigger = item.querySelector('[data-theinmag-legal-trigger]');
      if (!trigger) return;
      closeAll();
      setOpen(trigger, true);
      window.requestAnimationFrame(function () {
        scrollToItem(item);
      });
    }

    openFromHash();

    /* In-page links from elsewhere in the document (or the same page)
       that target an accordion anchor: open + scroll to it. */
    document.addEventListener('click', function (evt) {
      var link = evt.target.closest && evt.target.closest('a[href*="#"]');
      if (!link) return;
      var href = link.getAttribute('href') || '';
      var hashIndex = href.indexOf('#');
      if (hashIndex === -1) return;
      var hash = href.slice(hashIndex + 1);
      if (!hash) return;
      var item = document.getElementById(hash);
      if (!item || !root.contains(item)) return;
      var trigger = item.querySelector('[data-theinmag-legal-trigger]');
      if (!trigger) return;
      evt.preventDefault();
      closeAll();
      setOpen(trigger, true);
      if (history.replaceState) history.replaceState(null, '', '#' + hash);
      window.requestAnimationFrame(function () { scrollToItem(item); });
    });

    /* Browser back/forward also re-evaluates the hash. */
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', openFromHash);
  })();
</script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "theINmag legal accordions",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "theinmag-legal-accordions-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Spacing"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_top",
      "label": "Top padding",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 160,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 32
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding_bottom",
      "label": "Bottom padding",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 160,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 64
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "accordion_item",
      "name": "Accordion item",
      "limit": 10,
      "settings": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "title",
          "label": "Title"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "id": "anchor_id",
          "label": "Anchor id (URL hash)",
          "info": "Used for deep-linking, e.g. /pages/.../#submission-terms. Lowercase, hyphens only, no spaces."
        },
        {
          "type": "checkbox",
          "id": "is_kid_version",
          "label": "Mark as kid version (yellow star)",
          "default": false
        },
        {
          "type": "richtext",
          "id": "content",
          "label": "Body content"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "theINmag legal accordions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "accordion_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The kid version (read this first)",
            "anchor_id": "kid-version",
            "is_kid_version": true,
            "content": "<p>A short version, written for kids. The grown-up version is below. Your parent or guardian needs to read that one too.</p><h3>What is this page?</h3><p>Sending IN your creation is a big deal. It could end up in a real magazine. So we wrote down the rules for how that works, and how we keep you safe while we do it.</p><h3>What we'll publish about you</h3><p>If your creation gets in:</p><ul><li>Your first name and the first letter of your last name (so \"Banjo P.\" not \"Banjo Patterson\")</li><li>Your age</li><li>Your town and state</li></ul><p>That's it. We don't publish your full name, your school, your address, your phone number, your email, your face, or anything else. Promise.</p><h3>What we need from your grown-up</h3><p>Before you send IN anything, a parent or guardian has to say yes. That's why the form has a tick box for them. They're saying it's okay for us to print your creation and share it on our website and social media.</p><h3>What you can send IN</h3><p>Stuff you made. Drawings, stories, photos, jokes, maths puzzles, comics, songs, anything creative. It has to be your own creation, in your own style.</p><p>You can absolutely be inspired by things you love. Drawing your version of a favourite character, or writing a story about something you saw on TV, or making art in the style of an artist you admire is all welcome. That's how creative people learn.</p><p>What you can't do is straight-up copy someone else's creation and call it yours. Tracing a picture from a book and submitting it, or copying a story word-for-word from somewhere else, isn't sending IN your creation. It's sending IN theirs.</p><h3>What you can't send IN</h3><ul><li>Anything someone else made (copied, traced, or word-for-word)</li><li>Anything mean about another person</li><li>Anything with full names, addresses, or phone numbers in it (yours or anyone else's)</li><li>Photos of people's faces. Not yours, not your friends, not your family. theINmag is about your creations, not about what anyone looks like. (Photos of objects, places, animals, your hands making something, all good.)</li></ul><h3>What if you change your mind?</h3><p>If you ever want your creation taken down, your parent or guardian can email us and we'll take it off the website and our social media. We can't un-print magazines that have already been sent to people's letterboxes, but we'll never publish it again after that.</p><h3>Will I get money for it?</h3><p>Nope, not directly. Here's the deal: theINmag has no ads. Zero. So when we sell prints or t-shirts or anything else with kid creations on them down the track, every single dollar goes back into the next magazine. That keeps the magazine ad-free and full of kid creativity instead of toy commercials.</p><p>If that doesn't sit right with your family, that's totally fair. Don't tick the box, don't send IN. We'll still think you're cool.</p>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "accordion_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Child Safety Policy",
            "anchor_id": "child-safety",
            "is_kid_version": false,
            "content": "<p>This is the most important section. We're a magazine made by kids, for kids. That comes with serious responsibility, and we take it seriously.</p><h3>Who we are and what we're committed to</h3><p>theINmag is run by Ryan G. and Tam B., both former teachers, currently travelling Australia in a van with our daughter Nora. Before we were publishers, we spent years in classrooms. We've held the duty of care that comes with kids in your charge. We bring that mindset to every part of theINmag.</p><p>We're committed to the safety, wellbeing and dignity of every child whose creation appears in our magazine, on our website, or on our social media channels. We have zero tolerance for child abuse of any kind, and we comply with Australian child safety law including the National Principles for Child Safe Organisations.</p><h3>What we publish about a child</h3><p>When a child's creation is published in the magazine, on our website, or on social media, we publish only:</p><ul><li>The child's first name and the initial of their surname</li><li>Their age at the time of submission</li><li>The town and state they live in</li></ul><p>We do not publish full names, schools, addresses, contact details, photos of the child themselves, or any other information that could identify a child outside their immediate community.</p><h3>No photos of kids' faces, ever</h3><p>This is one of our core pillars. We do not publish photos of children's faces, anywhere, on any channel. Not in the magazine, not on the website, not on social media. The focus is the kid's creation, not what they look like. Photographs that include a child's face are returned and not published. Photographs of objects, places, or hands at work are welcome.</p><h3>What parental consent looks like</h3><p>We require explicit consent from a parent or legal guardian before any child's creation is published. That consent is captured at the point of submission, on the Send IN form, with a single tick box that covers:</p><ul><li>Permission to publish the creation in the printed magazine</li><li>Permission to display it on our website</li><li>Permission to share it on our social media channels</li><li>Permission to use it in our marketing and advertising</li><li>Permission to use it commercially (the full picture is in the Submission Terms section below)</li></ul><p>If a parent doesn't tick the box, the creation doesn't get published. End of story.</p><h3>What we will never do</h3><ul><li>We will never publish a child's full name, school, address, phone number, email, or face</li><li>We will never sell a child's personal information to anyone, ever</li><li>We will never publish a creation without parental consent</li><li>We will never run advertising in our magazine or on our kid-facing pages</li></ul><h3>Reporting a concern</h3><p>If you ever have a concern about anything related to a child's safety on our platform (content you've seen, behaviour you've noticed, an interaction with our team, anything at all), please email <a href=\"mailto:heyhey@theinmag.com.au\">heyhey@theinmag.com.au</a> and we will respond within two business days.</p><p>If a concern relates to suspected criminal conduct involving a child, we will report it to the relevant Australian authorities (Police, eSafety Commissioner, or the Department of Communities in the relevant state) in line with our mandatory reporting obligations.</p><h3>Asking us to take a creation down</h3><p>A parent or legal guardian can ask us to remove their child's creation at any time. Email <a href=\"mailto:heyhey@theinmag.com.au\">heyhey@theinmag.com.au</a> with the request and the issue or page it appears on, and we will:</p><ul><li>Remove it from our website within 7 days</li><li>Remove it from our active social media channels within 14 days</li><li>Stop using it in any future marketing or advertising</li></ul><p>What we cannot do: recall printed magazines that have already been distributed, or remove screenshots or shares that other people have made of our content. We're sorry. The print magazine is real and physical, and once it's in the world we can't pull it back.</p><h3>Annual review</h3><p>We review this Child Safety Policy every 12 months and whenever Australian child safety law changes. The \"Last reviewed\" date at the top of this page tells you when it was last looked at.</p>"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "accordion_item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Submission Terms",
            "anchor_id": "submission-terms",
            "is_kid_version": false,
            "content": "<p>These are the rules for sending IN a creation. If you're under 18, your parent or legal guardian needs to read this and tick the consent box on the Send IN form before you submit anything.</p><h3>Who can send IN</h3><ul><li>Any kid creator can send IN, anywhere in the world, as long as they have parent or guardian consent</li><li>The creation must be the kid's own work, in their own style</li><li>We accept submissions from kids of all ages</li></ul><h3>Original work, in your own style</h3><p>Kids are inspired by the world around them, and that's exactly how creativity works. Drawing a kid's version of a favourite character, writing a story sparked by a film, painting in the style of an artist they love, or photographing a scene that reminded them of something they've seen, all of that is welcome and encouraged. Kid-style versions of the things you love are how creative people learn.</p><p>What we don't accept: direct copies, traced images, or content reproduced word-for-word from another source and submitted as the kid's own. There's a clear line between inspiration and copying, and we trust kids and parents to know the difference. If we receive something that looks like a direct copy, we won't publish it.</p><h3>What you're agreeing to when you tick the consent box</h3><p>When a parent or legal guardian ticks the consent box on the Send IN form, they're giving theINmag permission to:</p><ol><li>Publish the creation in our printed magazine, on our website, on our social media, and in any other format we publish in now or in the future.</li><li>Use the creation in our advertising and marketing. That includes website banners, email newsletters, social media ads, school pitch decks, and anything else we use to grow theINmag.</li><li>Use the creation commercially. This is the broad bit, so we want to be clear about it. We may, now or in the future, sell products that feature kid creations, including prints, cards, calendars, books, merchandise, or anything else we choose to make. Every dollar of profit from those products goes straight back into the next print issue of theINmag, which is how we keep the magazine 100% ad-free. No proceeds go to the families of contributors.</li><li>Adapt or edit the creation for clarity, layout, spelling, or production needs. We won't change the meaning of a kid's creation. We may clean up a typo or crop an artwork to fit a page.</li><li>Sublicense the creation to printers, manufacturers, and other partners who help us produce the magazine and any related products.</li></ol><p>This is a non-exclusive, royalty-free, worldwide, perpetual licence. Plain English version: the kid still owns their creation. They can do whatever else they want with it. We just have permission to use it the ways listed above, anywhere in the world, for as long as we exist, without paying a royalty.</p><h3>Why the licence is broad</h3><p>We need to plan for the future. theINmag might one day sell art prints made from kid drawings to fund the next print run. We might licence creations into a school resource. We might run a workshop where kid creations get printed onto materials. We don't know everything we'll do in five years. The broad licence means we don't have to chase down hundreds of families every time we have a new idea, and every dollar from any of it goes back into the magazine.</p><p>If a parent isn't comfortable with that, we completely understand. The right move is to not tick the box, and not send IN. No hard feelings.</p><h3>What we won't accept</h3><p>We reserve the right to reject or remove any submission that:</p><ul><li>Isn't the kid's own creation</li><li>Includes content we consider unsafe, inappropriate, or harmful</li><li>Includes a recognisable photo of any child's face</li><li>Includes identifiable information about other kids who haven't given consent</li><li>Infringes someone else's copyright, trademark, or other rights</li><li>Doesn't fit the magazine for editorial reasons</li></ul><p>We don't owe you a reason if we choose not to publish a particular creation. We get a lot of submissions and we publish the ones that fit best for each issue.</p><h3>Withdrawing a submission</h3><p>A parent or legal guardian can withdraw consent at any time by emailing <a href=\"mailto:heyhey@theinmag.com.au\">heyhey@theinmag.com.au</a>. Once we receive a withdrawal request:</p><ul><li>We'll remove the creation from our website and active social channels (timeframes in the Child Safety section above)</li><li>We won't include the creation in any new printings or new products from the date of the request</li><li>We can't recall any printed magazines or products already distributed</li></ul>"
          }
